[
    {
        "id": "lp100aws-flow",
        "type": "tab",
        "label": "LP100A-WS",
        "disabled": false,
        "info": "WebSocket variant of the LP-100A dashboard. Talks to the\nlp100a-server (https://github.com/VU3ESV/LP-100A-Server) over\n`/ws` instead of opening the meter's COM port directly.\n\nEdit the `lp100aws-ws-client` config node to point at your server\n(default: ws://localhost:8088/ws). The serialport, parsing, range\ndeadbands, and alarm-trip logic are all handled server-side, so this\nflow is dramatically simpler than LP100A.json.\n",
        "env": []
    },
    {
        "id": "lp100aws-cmt-info",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "lp100aws-flow",
        "name": "About this flow",
        "info": "Source: lp100a-server (Go) at ws://<host>:8088/ws.\nIncoming JSON:\n  { type:\"telemetry\", seq, ts, data:{ power_w, z_ohm, phase_deg,\n     swr, dbm, dbw, range, mode, alarm_setpoint, alarm_tripped,\n     callsign } }\n  { type:\"heartbeat\", seq, ts }\n  { type:\"status\", level, msg }\n  { type:\"ack\", ref, ok }\n\nOutgoing JSON (control):\n  { type:\"command\", id, action:\"alarm_step\"|\"mode_step\"|\"peak_toggle\" }\n\nThis flow drops the Node-RED-side serial parsing because the server\nalready: parses the 9-field CSV, suppresses jitter (per-field deadbands\nin internal/meter/snapshot.go), converts dBm→dBW, computes alarm-trip,\nand maps range/mode/alarm bytes to readable names.\n",
        "x": 130,
        "y": 60,
        "wires": []
    },
    {
        "id": "lp100aws-cmt-revhist",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "lp100aws-flow",
        "name": "Rev history",
        "info": "Adapted from LP100A.json (serial flow by LB9KJ).\nWebSocket port: 2026-05-08.\n",
        "x": 110,
        "y": 100,
        "wires": []
    },
